## v0.26.0

### MCP-MISC-1 — leaf --format forwarded on typed tools, canonical --bridge-id emitted, scratch env/cwd on the generic CLI lane (SM-08, SM-10, SM-11; #421)

- Generated typed tools now forward leaf-declared output-format options:
  ``format``/``output``/``output_format`` were removed from the generator's
  skip list, so ~90 tools gained a ``format``/``output_format`` parameter
  (Literal-typed from the leaf's Choice). ``OUTPUT_FORMAT_OPTION``'s twin
  ``--output``/``--format`` Click options sharing one param name are deduped
  to a single parameter, ``--format`` preferred. The global ``--output=json``
  still precedes the group; a leaf option wins where the leaf honours it, and
  a non-JSON response simply yields no parsed ``json`` field in the envelope.
- ``get_flag_name`` now prefers the first-declared long option instead of the
  longest: ``crosschain bridge-status`` emits ``--bridge-id`` (the canonical
  spelling the CLI epilog and scenarios use) rather than the ``--transfer-id``
  alias. It was the only multi-long-option leaf in the tree.
- ``run_aitbc_cli`` and ``read_aitbc_cli`` accept ``env``/``cwd`` for scratch
  plays: env names are allow-listed (``HOME`` or non-secret ``AITBC_*``
  names), every value must be a path under ``/var/tmp/``, and ``cwd`` must be
  under ``/var/tmp/``. Values travel as a POSIX ``cd <dir> && KEY=value``
  prefix in the remote command string (SSH SendEnv is not used); the
  mutating-command gate is unchanged.
- Bug fixed on the local exec path: ``_run_remote`` previously ran
  ``shlex.split(command)`` directly, so a baked ``KEY=value`` prefix or
  ``cd <dir> &&`` clause became argv[0] and any env-using tool failed with
  FileNotFoundError on the MCP host's own node. The local branch now peels
  POSIX prefixes off the string and applies them as real child env/cwd.



### PLAY2-ERRORS-1 — classify and fix the errors the scenario-play C0 replay left (CLI + bridge monitor; #437)

- Phase 0 classified six replay errors: `ai status`/`results` 404 and the
  `zk`/`tee` 401s are correct behaviour (placeholder id, service-level
  auth); `ai service test` is correctly gated on MCP (it POSTs);
  `test_cli_basic` archive failures are environmental.
- Fix: `resource status` called ``client.get()`` which raises ``TypeError``
  on the ``/v1/agent-performance/resources`` JSON array before its own
  list-normalisation ran — now uses ``get_json()``; canned-shape tests in
  ``cli/tests/test_resource_status.py``.
- Fix (cosmetic): ``bridge_monitor``'s "broadcast as" log printed the
  derived envelope hash while the chain stores the RPC-returned hash —
  it now logs ``posted``. Service code; deploy needs a hub restart.



### PLAY3 — escrow create retries on the settlement node (CLI; #438)

- ``market transcribe``/``process``/``run`` escrow creation failed on
  customer nodes: ``/rpc/escrow/create`` is posted to the local blockchain
  RPC, which refuses settlement-shaped ops without a settlement key
  (``503``, "the settlement node is at <url>"). The command now retries
  the create once on the settlement URL the node names — the same refusal
  is already encoded, so a retry can only help a wrong-routed caller.
- ``_settlement_node_url`` parses the URL out of the node's detail text;
  tests stub the 503 + settlement-node path in
  ``cli/tests/test_escrow_settlement_fallback.py``.



### PG-TARGET-1 (stream 3) — disposable PostgreSQL cluster fixture; pool-hub + trading run real PG migrations (#436)

- Docker/podman are absent on the nodes, so the testcontainers fixture in
  `tests/conftest_testcontainers.py` never ran there. New
  `aitbc.testing.pg_cluster` spins a throwaway cluster from the installed
  server binaries (`initdb`/`pg_ctl` from PATH or
  `/usr/lib/postgresql/*/bin`) into a `/var/tmp` dir, bound to a unix socket
  only (`listen_addresses=''`), run as the `postgres` user when tests run as
  root, stopped and deleted at teardown — the running service and `aitbc_*`
  databases are never touched. `assert_local_pg_url` fails closed on any
  non-local DSN (netloc or `host=` query param), so the fixture cannot be
  pointed at a real database.
- `aitbc.testing.pytest_pg` exposes `postgres_cluster`/`pg_url` fixtures;
  suites opt in with `pytest_plugins` and `requires_postgres` skips cleanly
  when binaries are missing.
- Proof: `apps/pool-hub/tests/test_pg_migrations.py` and
  `apps/trading/tests/test_pg_migrations.py` run `alembic upgrade head`,
  a real insert, a real read and `downgrade base` on the throwaway cluster;
  `tests/test_pg_cluster.py` covers the URL guard and raw insert/read.
- Found by that proof run: `c3b2d6e7f8a9_add_poolhub_indexes` re-creates
  four indexes (`ix_sla_violations_{miner_id,created_at}`,
  `ix_sla_metrics_miner_id`, `ix_feedback_miner_id`) that earlier revisions
  already build — `if_not_exists` on upgrade, but both downgrades then
  dropped them unconditionally, so `downgrade base` died on PostgreSQL with
  UndefinedObjectError at the second drop. `if_exists=True` added to the
  duplicated drops in `c3b2d6e7f8a9`, `b2a1c4d5e6f7` and `a58c1f3b3e87`.
- Same finding in trading: `002_v0180_decimal_money_fields.downgrade()` ran
  its `ALTER COLUMN … TYPE FLOAT` unguarded, but three of its four tables
  are created by revision 003 — already dropped by `downgrade base` before
  002 runs. The downgrade now applies upgrade()'s inspector guards
  symmetrically.



## v0.25.13

### BRIDGE-MONITOR-1: bridge-monitor misses sealed deposits while idle and SUBMITTED rows never flip after seal. Poll-loop watchdog (BRIDGE-MONITOR-1): every cycle now logs a heartbeat ("poll cycle done in Xs"), the block scan is bounded by BRIDGE_CYCLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS (default 300, cursor holds and ALERTs on breach), a cycle exceeding the limit logs ALERT, and a frozen ETH head (stale LB backend answering fine but never advancing — the shape of the 25 Oct miss) alerts after BRIDGE_HEAD_STALL_SECONDS (600). ETH_RPC_TIMEOUT (10) makes the provider request timeout tunable. Status lag: the retry path stored the *derived* envelope hash in ait_tx_hash and never adopted the hash the RPC actually returned, so the seal probe keyed on a hash that can never seal — deposits stayed SUBMITTED after the payout landed (P8/C2). Both submit paths now share _adopt_posted_hash, which stores the chain-reported hash; envelope_hash keeps the derived anchor. Refs #448


## v0.25.12

### CLI: `exchange-island`/`market`/`wallet` read the array-shaped `/rpc/transactions` via `get_json`

The endpoint returns a bare JSON array; `AITBCHTTPClient.get()` rejects
non-object bodies, so `exchange-island orderbook`/`orders`/`rates`,
`market` offer resolution's chain fallback, and `wallet transactions`
all failed fleet-wide before reaching their (already shape-tolerant)
normalizers. Ops fix on hub: `island_credentials.json` `rpc_endpoint`
now `http://127.0.0.1:8202/rpc` — the previous `https://hub.aitbc.bubuit.net`
value hairpin-failed from inside the hub (fulfils the ops item CLI-3
deferred).


### Ops: bridge-leaf-audit reads `target_recipient` from sealed envelopes (AUDIT-RECIP-1). Sealed lock envelopes carry the recipient as `target_recipient` (`bridge_transfer.py`); the audit's `derived_leaf` only read `recipient`, so every lock sealed at/above `bridge_lock_leaf_v2_height` derived `unreadable` and classified `missing_data` — a false alarm, not a chain defect (LEAF-V2-LOCK-1 proved the v2 leaf correct end-to-end). `derived_leaf` now accepts the alias in payload and envelope; the skipped-trie line explains the `PYTHONPATH` requirement.


### CLI: `bridge proof`/`sign-proof`/`ingest-header`/`attest` no longer crash on the success line (CLI-PROOF-OUT-1). Each bound `--output` to a parameter named `output`, shadowing the `output()` formatter — the command wrote its file then raised `'str' object is not callable` (on `sign-proof` without `-o`, `None(...)` — unconditional). The options now bind to `output_path`; flag names unchanged. CliRunner tests pin the success path for `proof` and `sign-proof`.


### CLI: `bridge unlock --wallet-name` signs the refund in-process, like `lock` (CLI-UNLOCK-SIGN-1). `unlock` previously required a raw `--signature` built outside the CLI; it now accepts `--wallet-name`/`--wallet-password` and signs `{transfer_id, sender, action:"unlock"}` with the wallet key — the exact dict the server verifies. `--signature` stays accepted; both-given and a sender/wallet-address mismatch are refused before any request. Signatures and keys are never printed.


### Bridge: the lock signature now carries `issued_at` + a single-use `nonce` (SEC-REPLAY-1). The six-field lock dict `{source_chain, target_chain, sender, recipient, amount, asset}` had no freshness or nonce, so a captured signed body replayed in a later second minted a new `transfer_id` (it derives from `time.time()`) — debiting the sender and fee again. Wallet-signed locks (`bridge lock --wallet-name`) now sign and send `issued_at` (within ±`_ADMIN_MAX_SKEW_SECS`) and a random 16-byte hex nonce, keyed `bridge-lock:<sender>:<nonce>` in the shared sqlite nonce store — replay of the same body is refused once spent, and any body carrying the new fields is always freshness+nonce-checked regardless of flags. Old-form locks stay accepted while `BRIDGE_LOCK_REQUIRE_REPLAY_GUARD` is unset (deployed-CLI compatibility); setting it refuses old-form locks (400). Raw `--signature` submissions stay old-form for the compat window — flip the flag only after callers move to `--wallet-name`. Six other handlers share the generic signing scheme; their audit table is the SEC-REPLAY follow-up list.


### Bridge: `POST /bridge/batch/lock` removed — it moved funds with no sender signature at all (BRIDGE-BATCHLOCK-AUTH-1). The route fed `{"transfers":[…]}` straight into `initiate_transfer` per item, debiting any named `sender` plus the unrefunded fee; the only barriers were nginx's non-GET allowlist on `/rpc/bridge/` and the amount cap. Nothing legitimate called it — no CLI command, MCP tool, or script used `BridgeClient.batch_lock` — so the route, `BridgeBatchRequest`, the client method, and `bridge_transfer.batch_lock` are deleted rather than retrofitted. `POST /bridge/batch/confirm` stays: it verifies a confirmer signature per item and moves no funds itself. A regression test asserts `POST /bridge/batch/lock` now 404/405s so the route cannot quietly return; if a real caller ever appears it comes back with per-item signatures from the start.


### Bridge: cross-chain lock/swap signatures gain the replay guard (SEC-REPLAY-2). `_lock_transfer` (`POST /swap`, `POST /cross-chain/bridge`) verified a fixed-field signature with no nonce or expiry — a captured body replayed as a new `transfer_id`, debiting the sender again, same flaw as the single lock. The SEC-REPLAY-1 helpers are lifted into `rpc/utils.py` (`apply_replay_guard_fields`, `check_signed_request_freshness`, `verify_signed_request_with_freshness`) and shared: guarded bodies sign `issued_at` (±300 s) + `nonce` keyed `crosschain-lock:<sender>:<nonce>` in the shared store and are always checked; old-form stays accepted until `CROSS_CHAIN_REQUIRE_REPLAY_GUARD` is set (default off). `aitbc crosschain swap`/`bridge` now sign the fields. `_ADMIN_NONCE_TTL_SECS` goes 600→660 so a nonce spent at the earliest acceptable instant still outlives the body's freshness window. Route tests run through TestClient (the real JSON path), not handler-with-dict. `agent_economics_auth` reviewed and left alone: `create_agent_stake`/`deploy_bounty` are idempotent on their ids, the rest are state-bound transitions or append-only memos — replay moves no funds.


### Bridge: follower-side `bridge_state_root` shadow check (BRIDGE-ROOT-FOLLOW-1). Imported blocks carrying a root were stored verbatim — a follower never re-derived what the sender attached, and equal block hashes prove header agreement, not root correctness. New flag-gated `cross_chain/bridge_root_shadow.run_shadow_check` (off by default, `BRIDGE_ROOT_SHADOW_CHECK`): at/above the V-4 boundary it re-derives the trie root from the sealed BRIDGE_LOCK txs only — payload first, `envelope` fallback for GAP-47 rows, never the local `CrossChainTransfer` table (a lagging table would manufacture false mismatches) — and compares; a mismatch logs `[BRIDGE-ROOT-SHADOW]` with height + 12-hex root prefixes and counts `bridge_root_shadow_mismatch_total` (`_checked_total`, `_underivable_total`, `_errors_total` alongside). Compare-only: never rejects, never stores a different root, blocks below 30000 skipped, flag-off returns before touching a tx. Enforcement is a later, separate decision.


### CLI-SRCCHAIN-1 — `bridge lock --wallet-name` signs the resolved chain ID

Without `--source-chain`, the wallet-signed path signed `""` for
`source_chain` while the `/bridge/lock` route verifies against the node's
own resolved chain ID — a correct signature answered 403. The wallet path
now resolves the node's chain ID once via `GET /rpc/proposer`, signs it,
and sends it in the body; an unresolvable node errors out instead of
signing an empty string. The raw `--signature` path is unchanged, and its
help text (plus the MCP `bridge_lock`/`source_chain` descriptions) now
states the truth: the signed `source_chain` must equal the body's value
when sent, else the node's resolved chain ID. Server-side verification and
defaults untouched. Repo-only: no rollout.


## v0.25.11

### Bridge operations program: guarded unlock, set monitoring, fleet cap, proposer drain (2026-10-07)

A controlled rollout closed out the bridge hardening program on the live fleet. The four-attester epoch-1 set registered on all five nodes; the hub `aitbc-bridge-set` textfile exporter now reports `possible_signers=4` to Prometheus (`9812a91d51` object-form `VALIDATOR_SET` parse; `04b56ef9bb` 0644 output + latest-epoch-never-decays freshness matching `bridge_validator.py`), with `BridgeSetCannotMeetThreshold`/`BridgeSetStale` loaded and inactive.

- `BRIDGE_LOCKS_PAUSED=false` went live on all five hosts after the preconditions held (`possible_signers=4` + the registered epoch-1 set); `BRIDGE_MAX_LOCK_AMOUNT=36000000000` (1,000 AIT per-lock cap) rides with it in every `blockchain.env` and running rpc environment. Restart order: node2, node0, node1, hub1, hub.
- The retired proposer/proposer-account residue was drained in one `TRANSFER` (`0x54b547da…f2c` — 35,999,913,326,931,922 units to live `escrow_fee_recipient` `0x34FFfAd1…`, sealed at block 38368, nonce 90), keeping the 36,000,000,000-unit reserve. The account's ~36T-unit float no longer sits on a genesis-era identity.
- **Live result**: all five hosts at `04b56ef9bb`, `bridge/health` reporting `bridge_max_lock_amount: 36000000000`, `bridge_validators` latest epoch 1 with 4 active rows fleet-wide, no firing alerts, `BRIDGE_LOCKS_PAUSED=false` everywhere. The B43 persistence audit confirmed no deploy/update script can clobber these values; this version adds them to `blockchain.env.open-island` and documents the ops surface.


### CLI: `aitbc exchange prepay deposit` — node-tagged Sepolia ETH deposits for the prepaid-BUY lane (PREPAY-1)

D2's end-to-end match needs a BUY backed by a prepaid ETH balance, and no
safe CLI path existed to send the `EXCHANGE_DEPOSIT:<node address>` tagged
Sepolia ETH deposit the exchange monitor credits — `wallet bridge deposit`
writes the AIT bridge tag instead. The new command closes that gap without
key workarounds:

- `aitbc exchange prepay deposit --amount-eth X --wallet NAME` prints the
  unsigned transaction (chain id, recipient, value, the tag text, gas
  estimate, effective cap) and never touches a password or key — that is
  the default. `--send` signs once with the wallet's own decrypted key and
  broadcasts exactly once; a failed broadcast is reported, not retried.
- The recipient is always the `deposit_address` advertised by the exchange's
  own `/v1/bridge/status` — never typed by the operator.
- The input-data tag is `EXCHANGE_DEPOSIT:<node address>` as UTF-8, exactly
  what `wallet_app.bridge.bridge_monitor._parse_exchange_deposit_tag`
  parses; the test suite round-trips the produced hex through the real
  parser.
- Refusals: non-Sepolia chain id (must be 11155111), wallet not a
  `registered` node, missing or mismatched registry `eth_address`,
  `--amount-eth` above the per-call cap (default 0.01 ETH, raised only by
  an explicit `--max-eth`), amount+gas over the wallet's Sepolia balance,
  and — amended on review — a missing or unparseable `eth_gasPrice`:
  `--send` refuses rather than signing a zero-price transaction the
  network would reject, and the balance check can no longer pass on an
  assumed-zero gas cost. Over-cap refusals name the flag.
- The generated MCP tool `aitbc_exchange_prepay_deposit` lands in the
  `deposit` destructive class: `dry_run`/`confirm`-gated with
  `destructive_hint`, same as the other send paths.

Tests: 15 items in `cli/tests/test_exchange_prepay_deposit.py` — tag
round-trip through the real monitor parser, `--print` reading no key
material, wrong-chain/unregistered/eth_address-mismatch/empty-board
refusals, cap refuse/pass with `--max-eth`, `--send` signing and
broadcasting exactly once with the tag inside the signed blob, and
insufficient-balance refusal. `tests/cli/test_exchange_command_names.py`
adds the exchange-group name-collision guard (AST scan plus runtime
resolution of `prepay deposit`) mirroring the market-group test.


