### CLEAN-1: minor residuals — settlement terminal marks, status NaN guard, CLI wallet safety, tooling

- `b2393aa68` — settlement monitor: a `below_fee` inbound lock is now marked terminally (one WARNING, no forever-retry) matching the owner-remainder path, and `_lock_refund_ref_id` fails closed to `None` on an empty/non-hex lock hash — the caller treats it as `duplicate` (operator review) instead of crashing the lane.
- `53cd7ca45` — `website/status.js`: `Date.parse(head.timestamp)` producing `NaN` no longer reaches `fmtAge`; an unparseable or future-skewed head renders `live` without an age claim instead of `stale NaN…`. No test exists — the repo has no JS engine in the test tier and a text-extraction unit test was rejected by the task.
- `34a6365fa` — `genesis init` exits non-zero when `--password*` is given without `--create-wallet` (it printed an error and returned 0). Wallet liquidity stake/unstake re-saves go through `_resave_wallet`: an encrypted wallet can never be written back without the resolved password — `_load_wallet` may resolve one internally that the caller never sees, so a missing `resolved_password` aborts rather than dumping the plaintext key under the `encrypted` flag. The same pattern remains in `wallet/basic.py` (3 sites) — follow-up.
- `983fd1c3c` — `exchange order my --wallet` resolves the address metadata-only via `_fund_wallet_address` (the helper `order fund` already uses): reads the plaintext `address` field of an encrypted wallet file with daemon fallback, never touching key material — the command is documented "address only — no signing" yet demanded a password path (15 Oct D4 live find).
- `5699a1dc7` — tooling: (a) `check-mypy-changed.sh` already honoured `PYTHON` on both paths via `repo-python.sh` since TEST-1 (`af1b2adaa`) — verified, no change. (b) `no_float_money` gains `TIME_TOKENS` (deadlines, timestamps, retry counters, durations) so a `dict[int, float]` of monotonic deadlines under a money-named identifier is no longer a false positive; `at`/`last` deliberately stay guarded (`balance_at`, `last_payment` are amounts), `# not-money:` remains the escape hatch. (c) the exchange contract test's `bridge_monitor` fixture evicts `sys.modules` entries resolved outside the checkout and asserts the import lands under `_REPO_ROOT` — a stale cache from the venv's `aitbc-root.pth` (`/opt/aitbc` on `sys.path`) would otherwise silently win the `sys.path` insert and monkeypatch would pin nothing.


### Bridge float windows labelled host-local (CLI-5 follow-up)

- `GET /v1/bridge/float` reported `windows.today`/`last_24h` with a naive `since` while the docstring called it "UTC" — the ledger stamps are naive host-local times and the hub runs Europe/Vienna, so the label lied. Each window now reports `since` with the host UTC offset attached plus a `timezone` field naming the zone; the DB comparison is unchanged (naive local, same as the stored stamps). `wallet bridge status --today` help and output name the host-local basis and surface the zone; the route docstring documents the DST edge — on a change day the calendar-day and rolling-24h boundaries can sit an hour apart. `wallet-openapi.json` and the generated wallet MCP module regenerated.


### Ops tooling: bridge preflight gains a sepolia profile (ETH-5)

- `50646b0f8` — `scripts/ops/bridge_mainnet_preflight.py` learns `--profile {mainnet,sepolia}` (default `mainnet`; that profile's behaviour, output and exit codes are unchanged). Under `sepolia` the same seven knobs are read against testnet expectations: `ETH_NETWORK` must be exactly `sepolia` (the oracle maps anything else — unset included — to the mainnet feed map), `BRIDGE_CONFIRMATIONS` must reach the code's own default depth (>= 3), `CHAINLINK_MAX_AGE_SECONDS` must be *set* and <= 259200 (the oracle's deliberately generous 3-day default for sparse Sepolia feeds — a configured value above it is looser than the code's own bound), `BRIDGE_MAX_PAYOUT_AIT` must be set, and a set `AIT_USD_FIXED_PRICE`/`AIT_EUR_FIXED_PRICE` reports PASS with an `INFO:` detail naming the pin — a fixed AIT price is by design on testnet. The header names the profile so a sepolia PASS can never be mistaken for mainnet readiness.
- Part A (live, read-only): running the sepolia profile against hub's real `EnvironmentFile` stack (`blockchain.env` → `-blockchain-secrets.env` → `node.env` → `aitbc-bridge-monitor.env`) reports **6/7 PASS** — `CHAINLINK_MAX_AGE_SECONDS` is unset in the live config (inherits the 259200 code default; the sepolia knob requires an explicit value). Recorded as a finding; no env file was edited. node2 runs no bridge-monitor unit, so it carries no equivalent stack.
- Part B: a synthetic staging env stacked in the real unit order reports mainnet **7/7 PASS**; removing each set knob fails exactly that knob (`ETH_NETWORK` also fails the derived feed). Covered by the staging tests in `tests/unit/test_bridge_mainnet_preflight.py`.


### BR-6c — monitor classifies sell-lock/settlement releases

- `release_sources()` maps each release-leg `eth_tx_hash` to its table
  (`eth_withdrawals`, `bridge_sell_locks`, `exchange_settlements`);
  `release_tx_hashes()` now covers all three. Missing tables tolerated.
- `_record_outbound` records those sends as `withdrawal` with the source
