### SEC-18: wallet bridge 5xx bodies drop config and exception text (2026-10-14)

`4bd1aca607` and `2f04b11160` fix three `bridge_routes.py` sites that put
server configuration or exception text into 503 response bodies — the
class `tests/security/test_http_exception_hardening.py` already forbids
(the wallet parametrization was red on main because nothing ran the full
suite). `_sell_gate` and the sell-quote config path return
`detail="Sell escrow disabled"` instead of `Sell escrow disabled:
<reason>`/`<exc>` — the removed text named the missing env keys
(`AIT_SELL_ESCROW`, `SELL_ESCROW_WALLET_PATH`, `CHAIN_ID`). The
admin-gated poll trigger returns `"Bridge poll skipped"` instead of
echoing the monitor's `"ETH_WALLET_ADDRESS not set"` reason. Each site
logs the removed text via `logger.warning` with `redact_text`, so a
keyed RPC URL cannot reach the journal either; the remaining 5xx
details were audited and are fixed constants (rate_info "error",
"Internal server error" + `logging.exception`). `wallet-openapi.json`
regenerated; the 503 descriptions lose the `{reason}`/`{exc}`
placeholders. Tests assert the fixed body and the logged config text
for all three sites.


### ETH-11 phase 2: signed orders + node registration on the hub exchange (2026-10-14)

Phase 2 of `docs/design/eth8-exchange-page.md` (decisions DECIDED 14 Oct):
the hub exchange now accepts only **signed** order envelopes from
registered nodes, still entirely unfunded — placement moves no funds and
a match books a `trades` row but settles nothing (funded settlement is
phase 3 / ETH-16, gated on a clean ETH-2 live withdraw run).

- `db.py`: `node_registry` (seeded from the fleet wallet labels), the
  exchange-scoped `order_nonces` counter (explicitly not the chain
  nonce), and order columns `signature`/`envelope_hash`/`account_nonce`/
  `expiry_height`/`funded_state`/`cancelled_at`; `orders.status` gains
  `expired` via a table-rebuild migration preserving existing rows;
  `trades` gains `buyer_address`/`seller_address`/`fee_ait`. The design's
  `prepaid_eth` table was deliberately left out — it is phase-3 funding
  machinery with no phase-2 readers.
- `handlers/exchange.py`: `keccak256(canonical JSON)` envelope verify via
  the repo's `signature_recovery`/`crypto` primitives before any DB
  write; per-IP rate limit runs *before* the secp256k1 verify; nonce
  replay guard with byte-identical resubmit dedupe via
  `(user_address, envelope_hash)`; expiry bound to chain head; ±10%
  `EXCHANGE_PRICE_BAND_PCT` oracle band (feed stale → 503 like the quote
  endpoint); `EXCHANGE_MIN_ORDER_AIT`; per-node and book-wide open-order
  caps; cancel shares the placement `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` lock so
  cancel-vs-fill is a deterministic 409 for the loser; self-match never
  fills; watchlist flag for cancel-ratio abuse — flag-and-log only
  (never blocks), knobs `EXCHANGE_CANCEL_RATIO` (0.9),
  `EXCHANGE_WATCHLIST_MIN_PLACEMENTS` (10), `EXCHANGE_WATCHLIST_WINDOW_S`
  (86400); per-IP limits read `X-Real-IP` only from peers in
  `EXCHANGE_TRUSTED_PROXIES` (default `127.0.0.1,::1`); all phase-2 rows
  stay `funded_state='unfunded'`. Chart feeds `depth`/`ohlc`/`stats`
  (design §8, no phase marker) are deferred — read-only follow-up, no
  schema gap (trades/orders tick columns already cover them).
- Routes: `POST /api/orders` (signed), `POST /api/orders/<id>/cancel`,
  `POST /api/exchange/nodes/register`, `GET /api/exchange/nodes`,
  `GET /api/exchange/my-orders?addr=`. The signature is the credential —
  `X-Api-Key` stays only on the legacy unsigned path, which remains
  reachable by pinning `EXCHANGE_REQUIRE_SIGNED_ORDERS=0` (flag-off also
  404s the phase-2 routes).
- CLI: `aitbc exchange order place|cancel|my` and
  `aitbc exchange node register`, signing with the node's own wallet via
  `load_wallet_for_payment` (`--password-file`/`--password-stdin`; no
  secrets in argv/logs). `chain_id` resolves fail-closed through
  `resolve_chain_id` against the node's own RPC. MCP wrappers
  regenerated.
- Docs: exchange API documented by hand in `docs/apps/exchange/
  exchange.md` — the service is a `BaseHTTPRequestHandler`, excluded
  from generated OpenAPI by design; scenario 59 gains a phase-2
  Scenario D addendum.


### ETH-JOIN: settlement join hardening — retryable order_funded refusals, order_refund ref_id split, flag-off 503 (2026-10-15)

Pre-enable review of the ETH-19 sell-lock ⇄ settlement join (report-only
pass, then this fix pass) found three places where a caller-visible or
transient condition could strand escrowed AIT or corrupt an unrelated row.
All three are fixed while the feature is still dark on every host.

- A non-terminal `order_funded` refusal (5xx, 401/403, the flag-off 503)
  no longer writes a `lock:<tx_hash>` monitor mark — the lock stays in the
  escrow scan and retries next poll, counted under
  `stats["http_refused"]` with a WARNING line per refusal. Previously the
  mark was written unconditionally (`outcome="http_500"`), so one
  transient refusal permanently skipped the lock: never funded, never
  refunded — `orders.backing_tx_hash` stays NULL, so the remainder lane
  never even sees it. 404/409 keep their terminal refund path; transport
  errors were already unmarked.
- `order_refund` `ref_id` convention: the order's own lock-remainder
  refund keeps `+order_id`; a refund for an inbound lock the order could
  not take keys on `−order_id`. A stranger's dust lock naming the order
  can no longer squat the UNIQUE(kind,ref_id) row the owner's remainder
  refund needs — the same collision also protected a second stray lock,
  which is still a logged duplicate. No schema or live-data migration:
  `ref_id` is a signed INTEGER and the CHECK on `kind` is untouched.
- `POST /api/internal/exchange-settlement` answers 503
  `{"error": "exchange settlement disabled"}` while the exchange-side flag
  is off — 404 was indistinguishable from "order not found" and mapped to
  a lock refund. The supported rollout order (exchange flag first, wallet
  flag second) is now in `docs/apps/exchange/exchange.md`; with both
  flags off nothing changes — the monitor does not post at all.

Adjacent hardening in the same diff: `_set_status_and_mirror` skips the
tape mirror for `kind='order_refund'` — refund rows have no trade row and
`ref_id` shares the order/trade integer space, so mirroring one could post
`trade_state` onto an unrelated trade (or 404-pointlessly every poll).

Commits: `0d77eec16` (wallet monitor + schema comment + tests),
`6ff2adfac` (exchange endpoint + flag-off test). `exchange.md` rollout
ordering lands in the accompanying docs commit.



### CLI-1: exchange-settlement CLI gaps — node list/set-payout, order fund, settlements view (2026-10-15)

The trading CLI covered orders and bridge basics but none of the
settlement surfaces that `EXCHANGE_SETTLEMENT_ENABLED` (still dark on
every host) introduces. Five additive pieces close the gap; flag-off
behaviour is byte-identical everywhere.

- `aitbc exchange node list` — the existing `GET /api/exchange/nodes`
  had no CLI counterpart. Read-only, no wallet or signature.
- `aitbc exchange node set-payout --eth-address` — signs the
  `exchange_set_payout` envelope against `POST /api/exchange/nodes/payout`,
  mirroring `node register` (account nonce from `my-orders`, island
  chain_id). The address is EIP-55-checksummed before signing because
  the exchange refuses anything else.
- `GET /api/exchange/my-orders` now attaches the `funding` block
  (escrow, `required_ait`, `payload`) to rows that are `open` AND
  `funded_state='unfunded'` — only while the settlement flag is on.
  The block previously existed only in the place-order response, so a
  node that lost it had no way to re-read the lock target. Shapes are
  extracted into a shared read-only `_funding_block` used by both the
  place path and my-orders; no schema change.
- `aitbc exchange order fund <id> [--print]` — resolves the order's
  funding block and sends the escrow-lock TRANSFER with payload
  `{"sell_quote": <id>}` through the normal transaction-signing path.
  `--print` emits the equivalent `aitbc transactions send` line and
  broadcasts nothing. Refuses BUY orders (ETH prepay is out of scope),
  funded/expired/cancelled rows, and flag-off exchanges. Implemented
  and tested against mocks only — the help text says so; a real AIT
  transfer is an operator-named move.
- `GET /v1/bridge/settlements` + `aitbc wallet bridge settlements` —
  the `exchange_settlements` ledger (trade / `order_refund` rows,
  `needs_review`, error text) had no HTTP surface; state/kind filters
  and bounded limit/offset pagination. The route is **admin-keyed**
  (`require_admin_api_key`, same stance as `sell/quote`): hub nginx
  exposes the whole `/v1/bridge` mount publicly and the rows carry
  diagnostic error text plus release nonces/hashes.

Also fixed: the `order` group help still claimed "unfunded,
settlement-free". MCP tool modules regenerated — `order fund` and
`node set-payout` land under `dry_run`+`confirm` guards, `node list`
is read-only. `docs/api/wallet-openapi.json` regenerated for the new
route. Out of scope: hub default exchange URL/nginx routing (separate
operator decision), BUY-side funding, ETH-JOIN review items 4-7.

Commits: `11135740c` (exchange my-orders funding block + tests),
`8605f6b21` (CLI commands, wallet route/db/tests, MCP regen, openapi).



### CLI-2: CLI/MCP tooling gaps — file-info, env-names, exchange verify, MCP optional-arg schema (2026-10-15)

Four tooling gaps reported from the ops/MCP sweep (TOPOLOGY
2026-10-15-cli-mcp-gaps.md): every one had been worked around with
ssh+python because the CLI either dumped too much or required too much.
All additions are read-only; the generic `run-script --interpreter/--arg`
extension is deferred to the gap log as a separate reviewed item — it
widens a remote-exec surface.

- `aitbc system file-info --path <f> [--field <name>]` — the safe form
  of `system file` (which dumps whole content, including a wallet
  JSON's private_key). Reports size, mtime, owner, mode, type and the
  JSON top-level key names; deliberately no sha256 — a hash of a short
  secret file is brute-forceable offline. `--field` prints one scalar
  top-level field under a hard, case-insensitive denylist (private_key,
  secret, password, token, mnemonic, seed, key, api_key, `*_key`, and
  the same names nested in the value); denied names and >200-char
  values are explicit refusals. The allowlist now resolves the real
  path — a symlink escaping the allowed prefixes is refused.
  `/var/lib/aitbc/wallets` and `/etc/aitbc/credentials` are covered;
  `/root/.aitbc/wallets` is not.
- `aitbc system env-names --service <unit> [--names K...]` — variable
  NAMES only from `systemctl show -p EnvironmentFiles` (drop-ins
  included, `-` optional prefix handled, `/etc/aitbc/<unit>.env`
  fallback kept); unparsed lines report file+line number, never text;
  `--names` reports set/unset. `env-set` behaviour unchanged.
- `aitbc exchange verify` — wraps `scripts/ops/exchange_verify.py`:
  `sys.executable` argv exec (no shell), `--no-chain` default unless an
  RPC is given, `--db`/`--bridge-db` must be absolute existing regular
  files without `..` or symlinks, exit code and FAIL/WARN lines pass
  through. The script is stdlib-only and opens sqlite `mode=ro`.
- Generated MCP wrappers annotated every optional CLI option `T | None`
  but emitted no `= None` default, so FastMCP marked them all required
  (the sweep passed explicit nulls to work around it). The generator
  now appends `= None`; all 76 groups regenerated. The three new
  commands land as read-only MCP tools (`FORCE_READ_ONLY_PATHS`).

Tests: a denied field is proven absent from stdout and logs; `system
file` dump behaviour unchanged; an omitted MCP optional never reaches
the command line nor the string "None"; verify argv/passthrough/path
refusals covered. Spec: tasks/batch-05-build/CLI-2 (director-approved
with five amendments, all folded in).

Commits: `4b214fdb2` (MCP schema fix + regen + None-skip test),
`77ebd3aaf` (file-info + env-names + tests + docs), `facb65ebc`
(exchange verify + tests + docs).



### OPS-6: exchange base default is the bare hub origin (2026-10-15)

`hub_exchange_url()` fell back to `hub_service_url("exchange")` —
`https://<hub>/exchange` — but hub's `/exchange/` prefix is deliberately
the wallet service's mount (exchange-deposit routes), so every node with
no explicit `exchange_service_url`/`HUB_EXCHANGE_URL` resolved the
signed-order surface to the wrong service and `aitbc exchange …` failed
without `--exchange-url`. The fallback is now the bare hub origin via a
new `hub_origin()` helper: the signed-order routes publish at their
natural `/api/*` paths (OPS-4 mounts them there). Explicit
`HUB_EXCHANGE_URL`/`EXCHANGE_SERVICE_URL`/config values still win —
nodes carrying the stale explicit `…/exchange` value are fixed by
OPS-5's per-node config step, not by this change.

Tests: bare-origin default, explicit-env precedence, and the updated
process-env assertion all pass in `test_hub_url_from_env.py`.

Commits: `b710f2574` (hub.py default + hub_origin + test + scenario doc).


### CLI-3: signed exchange ops resolve the envelope chain-id — /rpc-suffix probe fix + island-creds fallback + `config get <key>` (2026-10-15)

Every signed exchange op (`node register`, `node set-payout`,
`order place`, `order fund`, `order cancel`) resolved its envelope
chain_id by probing the node's RPC — `get_chain_id_from_health` tried
`<base>/rpc/proposer` then `<base>/health` expecting a ROOT base, while
`get_rpc_endpoint()` and node `blockchain_rpc_url` config hand it
`/rpc`-suffixed bases (`https://<hub>/rpc`). The probes hit
`/rpc/rpc/proposer` → 404 → `ChainIdLookupError` on every island host;
node2's first `set-payout` needed `--chain-id ait-hub.aitbc.bubuit.net`
to get through. The probe now strips one trailing `/rpc` before building
its client, so bare and suffixed bases resolve identically — this also
fixes the same latent failure in the `ai.py`, `agent_sdk.py`,
`gpu_resources.py` and `market/gpu.py` signing paths that feed
`resolve_chain_id` the same bases.

`_envelope_chain_id` additionally falls back to the island credentials'
`island_chain_id` (the required field written and validated at join)
when no node answers the probe — covers hosts whose only RPC endpoint
is unreachable. Explicit `--chain-id`/`CHAIN_ID` still win, the strict
probe still runs for hosts without credentials, and the fail-closed
abort is last — never a silent default.

`aitbc config get <key>` is now a real subcommand (it was an alias for
`config show` that ignored the key — OPS-5 had to read yaml via ssh
grep): prints the stored value of one key, exits nonzero when absent or
the file is missing, refuses the file-info denylist plus
passphrase/credential, and keeps bare `config get` as the show alias.
`--global` reads the global config file. MCP wrapper regenerated for the
new signature.

Commits: `e64b0b123` (probe normalization + creds fallback + tests),
`9e7daa873` (config get + tests + MCP regen).


### CLI-4: `exchange order fund` forwards the wallet password options (2026-10-15)

`order fund` declared `--password/--password-file/--password-stdin` but
passed none of them to `load_wallet_for_payment`, so an encrypted wallet
aborted "No wallet password available" before the funding block was read —
`--print` included (found live on node2 during ETH-19 Phase D1; the D1
lock went out via `transactions send` instead). The loader cannot return
an address without decrypting (`_load_wallet` decrypts eagerly and its
password resolution is unconditional), so the command now reads the
wallet's plaintext `address` field — or the daemon record — for both
paths, then resolves the password exactly once right before the broadcast
and reuses it for `_send_transaction_impl`: `--password-stdin` is
consumed one time, `--print` on an encrypted wallet needs no password at
all, and a failed send exits nonzero instead of silently skipping the
success line. Sender-must-be-order-owner, the funding-block aborts, the
`{"sell_quote": <id>}` payload and the lock amount are unchanged.

Tests (`TestOrderFundEncryptedWallet`) run a real `_save_wallet`-encrypted
fixture through real password resolution with only the broadcast mocked:
`--password-file` reaches the send, wrong and missing passwords fail
closed, `--print` works with empty stdin, and `--password-stdin` is read
exactly once. The existing fund tests move from the loader mock to a real
unencrypted wallet file.

Commits: `f8933199e2` (password forwarding + plaintext address read +
real encrypted-wallet tests).


### ETH-JOIN-2: settlement join-review gaps 4–7 + order_refund squat residual (2026-10-15)

Second pass on the ETH-JOIN report, closing the four low-severity items
left open by ETH-JOIN (gaps 1–3) plus the residual it documented against
the `order_refund` ref_id sign split. All monitor-side; no schema change
and no live deployment — the code ships on the next authorized pull.

- **Gap 4 — normalized lock-hash compares.** `orders.backing_tx_hash` vs
  observed `tx_hash` was an exact-string equality at two sites: the
  re-observe check in `_order_lock_outcome` and the sender lookup in
  `_lock_sender`. An RPC case/prefix respelling would have dropped a
  funded lock into the refund lane, and on the sender path made
  `_lock_sender` fall back to `user_address` — refunding a stranger's
  lock remainder to the order owner. `_norm_tx_hash` (lower, strip `0x`)
  now normalizes both sides at both sites.
- **Gap 5 — no-payout trade skips and self-heals.** A trade whose seller
  had no `seller_eth_address` (order snapshot NULL *and* node_registry
  NULL) threw inside the ETH-release lane, pinning the settlement row in
  `needs_review` forever. `_ensure_trade_rows` now skips the trade before
  a row exists — retried each poll, so a seller who registers a payout
  address later settles on the next pass. The skip counts as
  `no_payout_skipped` in the poll summary and logs ERROR at most once
  per trade per hour (DEBUG between) so it cannot flood the journal.
- **Gap 6 — unlinked trades get a terminal mark.** Trades with NULL
  `buy_order_id`/`sell_order_id` logged an ERROR every poll forever with
  no terminal state. They now take a `unlinked-trade:<id>` monitor mark
  (detail carries the NULL links, `outcome="no_order_links"`) — one
  ERROR line at discovery, skipped silently afterwards. Kept the
  monitor-mark path rather than stamping `failed`: `failed` has no
  incoming edge in either `_TRADE_STATE_FORWARD` map, and adding one
  would have changed ETH-JOIN's mirror semantics.
- **Gap 7 — hash-domain comment.** `orders.tx_hash`/`backing_tx_hash`
  are 0x-prefixed chain transaction hashes; `envelope_hash` is the
  0x-less keccak of the signed order envelope — the shared word "hash"
  now carries an explicit never-compared comment in
  `simple_exchange/db.py`. Comment only.
- **Squat residual — owner-priority lock-keyed fallback.** ETH-JOIN's
  `−order_id` inbound-refund row can still be squatted by a stranger's
  dust lock: the owner's later under-cover lock then collided on UNIQUE
  and landed as `duplicate` needing a manual refund. When the
  `−order_id` slot is held, an *owner*-sender lock (the same
  `_sender_owns_order` check used for crediting) now falls back to
  `_lock_refund_ref_id(tx_hash)` — `−(int(hash[:15],16) | 2**59)`,
  deterministic per lock so a re-scan dedupes, bounded below `−2**59`
  so it cannot collide with any `±order_id` or trade id. Stranger
  duplicates stay bounded at one inbound-refund row per order — the
  dust-spam fee drain stays closed. A UNIQUE collision on the derived
  id still returns `duplicate` for operator review rather than crashing.
  Reader audit: `ref_id` is exact-match everywhere (`get_exchange_settlement`
  callers pass `±order_id` only; the mirror/refund lanes never read
  `order_refund` ref_ids; `exchange_verify.py`, routes, CLI and MCP are
  display-only), so the third ref_id class is opaque to every consumer.
  The `exchange_settlements` schema comment now documents all three
  classes. A `lock_tx_hash`-keyed column remains the cleaner long-term
  cut — left as a proposal, not implemented (no schema change was in
  scope).

Tests: 8 new cases in `test_exchange_settlement_monitor.py` — hash
normalization at both sites, derived-ref_id range/idempotence over
multiple hashes, owner-under-cover refund surviving a stranger squat,
second stranger lock still `duplicate`, re-poll writing no second row,
unlinked-trade terminal mark, no-payout skip counting and self-healing,
poll-summary counter. All failed before the source change; the full
65-test monitor suite, wallet suite, exchange suite, ruff, mypy
ratchet and check-c901-ratchet pass.

Commits: `8f9ac9f06` (monitor + schema comment + tests),
`88f0b3812` (exchange hash-convention comment).


### ETH-19B: the internal settlement endpoint accepts its loopback caller (2026-10-15)

`FastAPIRequestAdapter.__init__` never called
`BaseHTTPRequestHandler.__init__` and never set `self.client_address`, so
`_raw_peer_ip()` read a missing attribute, returned `""`, and
`_peer_is_loopback()` failed closed — `POST
/api/internal/exchange-settlement` answered 403 to every caller,
including the wallet settlement monitor's `order_funded` post for the
sealed D1 lock (found live on hub 15 Oct; the monitor correctly left the
row unmarked and retried each poll). The existing tests missed it
because they assign `client_address` on a fake handler.

The adapter now sets `client_address` from the ASGI request's client
(`("", 0)` when absent so the gate still fails closed), and `server.py`
runs uvicorn with `proxy_headers=False`. Evidence for that choice:
uvicorn 0.49.0's `ProxyHeadersMiddleware` rewrites `scope["client"]`
from `X-Forwarded-For` whenever the immediate peer is trusted (default
`127.0.0.1`, widenable via `FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS`) — a spoofable value
for a wire-peer gate; and an in-app ASGI wrapper cannot recover the
pre-middleware peer because uvicorn applies its middleware outside the
loaded app. The endpoint's second gate (refuse any request carrying
`X-Forwarded-For`/`X-Real-IP`) already made the composition safe, but
the raw peer removes the dependency entirely. The app's only other
`request.client` consumer is the `_client_ip` rate limiter, which
prefers nginx's `X-Real-IP` under its own `EXCHANGE_TRUSTED_PROXIES`
list — identical results with the rewrite off, verified by audit.

New tests in `test_internal_settlement.py` drive the real
`_dispatch`/adapter path through `TestClient(client=(host, port))`:
loopback + key + valid `prepaid_credit` → 200 with the deposit row
applied; loopback without key → 401; `10.1.2.3` → 403;
`::ffff:127.0.0.1` → 200 (IPv4-mapped loopback); loopback carrying
`X-Forwarded-For` or `X-Real-IP` → 403; a scope with no client → 403;
flag off → 503; and TestClient's `("testclient", 50000)` default is
documented as refused. A per-test fixture refreshes
`main._HANDLER_POOL` — `test_http_contract`'s real-uvicorn fixture shuts
it down on teardown, so the new tests are suite-order-proof.

Commits: `106987e87` (adapter client_address + proxy_headers=False +
real-adapter gate tests). Deploy/restart of the hub exchange is a
separate gated step — until then the running service still 403s.



## v0.25.10

### Escrow settlement pipeline: fee-sweep pass, owed-change pass, release-route changes, billed-legs proof, alerts, signing guard (A4–A7, S-8)
All sweeper work lives in the settlement-mark sweeper's tick and submits at most one transaction per pass through the existing guarded submission paths. Proof source is **sealed chain legs**, never row columns.

- **Fee-sweep pass (A4)** — `ffa8ba1811` adds the periodic pass: the route-emitted `ESCROW_FEE_SWEEP` was unreachable (same sealed-nonce slot lost to the higher-fee release; retry skipped `refunded_amount NULL` rows — both live sweeps had been hand-signed). The pass derives residue `lock − Σ releases − Σ refunds − Σ sweeps` from sealed legs and gates fail-closed on: fee-only proof (residue inside the withheld-fee bound), exact custody equality, proposer-mempool pending guard (nothing submits while any authority/ESCROW tx is pending or the mempool is unprobeable), grace (newest leg older than `escrow_fee_sweep_pass_grace_seconds`, default 300 s), a height floor (`escrow_fee_sweep_pass_min_height`, default **35400** — pre-v11 rows are the operator's custody-census decision), and `escrow_fee_sweep_pass_max_jobs` (50/pass). `ESCROW_FEE_SWEEP_PASS_ENABLED` gates it; `_DRY_RUN` logs without submitting; new counter `blockchain_escrow_fee_sweep_pass_total{result}`. Follow-ups `b822dfcc17 ab6af01226 c8da7450d0 955558803d 2c3e55eb1f 91592c3aa8 bba05473aa c1fb316cb6 5489018319 030ee5c6aa`: config plumbing, dry-run/alerts/`skipped_floor` reason, counters + deploy/rollback docs, protected-row deferral, custody-equality proof-of-life check, per-pass job bound, and zero-value refund legs kept out of the residue sum (v11's post-refund guard row).
- **Release route + owed-change pass (A6/F1b)** — `45a1b81b86`: the route stops signing a change leg (an in-request leg loses the same `(sender, nonce)` mempool slot; a waited-for leg outlives every caller timeout). It submits the release only, commits unmarked, and answers `change_owed_amount`. `208b278f28`: the sweeper's change pass pays owed change on a later tick — flag-gated (`ESCROW_CHANGE_PASS_ENABLED`), dry-run capable, per-job backoff, same pending-guard/custody-equality rules, heal-first marking from a sealed refund leg, and it runs **before** the fee pass so a submitted refund trips the fee pass's own guard — refund and sweep never share a mempool window. `694fdeaac6 c906ab7fb5 411e130c15`: sweeper heal-pass demotion of stale unsealed release marks, env-template/fleet-config-check/alert updates, and the tx-hash-pairing gap (an orphan mark from a dropped re-drive no longer blocks the row).
- **Billed-legs proof (A7)** — `305f2a6b70`: `Escrow.billed_legs` records the route's billed gross keyed by tx_hash; `recompute_release_proofs()` replays the route's own fee rule (protected rows bumped to the signed provider credit; multi-leg jobs refuse). Fee pass, change pass, and the route's sweep retry all gate on the recompute — the inversion bound is removed (it only ever proved round-number synthetic jobs). Legacy `billed_legs NULL` rows stay `deferred_unproven`. `d904db2d64 6e16ddb1af fb6fba7dc5 66fd8c8a6f`: protected-row release sealed-value probe, residue min(), floor under A7, release-side tx-hash pairing fix. `2d69e579ac`: heal `refunded_amount`/`swept_amount` from sealed legs and split `deferred_change_pending` out of `deferred_unproven`. `a72ec34ac2`: energy-floor bump from the signed provider credit.
- **Settlement-signing guard + refusal counter** — `29455df496`: `create_escrow` and `refund_escrow` refuse 503 (nothing submitted, no row) on a node that cannot sign settlement — a follower lock strands escrow the hub cannot see; detail names the settlement node when `HUB_RPC_URL` is set, and followers answer the hub-hint 503 on duplicate creates; the shared predicate `_settlement_signing_ready()` is also called by the release gate. `b1c5ceaaa6`: refusal counter `aitbc_escrow_settlement_refused_total{op}` (op ∈ create/refund/release) incremented at each refusal. `dc40c48599`: pre-creates the counter's three op children at 0 so the very first refusal fires `increase()` instead of appearing as a new series. `272103b18a`: renames the counter into the `blockchain_` prefix family — `blockchain_escrow_settlement_refused_total{op}`. `cd3b0af92c`: the two alerts — `EscrowSettlementRefusedOnHub` (never-event, `scripts/monitoring/aitbc_hub_rules.yml`) and `EscrowSettlementRefusedOnFollower` (`scripts/monitoring/aitbc_rules.yml`). `92fa423b61`: the CLI surfaces the node's 503 detail on escrow create/refund/release (create no longer reports success to scripts on a refusal).
- **Monitoring/tests** — `bc79119eec`: settlement-detector crash on missing tx. `86adfaf9b9`: `check-first-sweep.py` one-off proof checker. `0bc2d73b75`: A6 route contract tests. Alert rules `d84c486934 dc9622b2ed 309dddbb1e` (split `EscrowFeeSweepPassUnprovenRows` vs `EscrowFeeSweepChangePending`, pass-tick staleness). `7cc552b26c`: stale xfail section header corrected.


## v0.25.9

### New bridge locks paused — BRIDGE_LOCKS_PAUSED (2026-09-29)

``POST /bridge/lock``, ``/bridge/batch/lock``, ``/swap`` and
``/cross-chain/bridge`` now return HTTP 503
``{"detail": "Bridge paused: locks unavailable until validator attestation is live"}``
while ``BRIDGE_LOCKS_PAUSED=true`` (``config.py: bridge_locks_paused``,
code default False; set true in ``/etc/aitbc/blockchain.env`` on all five
hosts — the env file the ``aitbc-blockchain-rpc`` unit already loads, so
the RPC process and the in-process bridge see the flag). Refund
(``/bridge/unlock``), status reads, confirm, batch-confirm and withdraw
are deliberately untouched: in-flight transfers stay refundable/releasable
while locks are paused.

Boundary chosen: the lock *request* path, not mempool admission. All four
REST surfaces funnel through ``CrossChainBridge.initiate_transfer``, which
now raises ``BridgeLocksPausedError`` (a ValueError) before any
debit/row/mempool write — the chokepoint also covers internal callers, so
no new validly-signed BRIDGE_LOCK envelope can be created while paused.
Admission stays open on purpose: nothing outside ``initiate_transfer``
can produce a BRIDGE_LOCK that seals anyway (v9 apply requires the bridge
authority ``bridge_signature`` — ``state/state_transition.py`` and the
pure path enforce it, and the proposer skips pre-registered locks whose
signature fails), a replayed envelope dedupes on ``tx_hash``, and — the
deciding point — a lock issued *before* the pause must still be able to
seal, because ``BRIDGE_REFUND`` binds a *sealed* lock since v6. Refusing
admission while paused would strand an in-flight lock's funds, which is
exactly what the pause exists to prevent.

Verified before enabling: ``cross_chain_transfer`` has zero pending/locked
rows on all five hosts (only confirmed + refunded), and no BRIDGE_LOCK
sits in any mempool — nothing in flight to drain.


